## <summary>qdistro Tier-2 SELinux container confinement.</summary>
## <desc>
##   A tightened sibling of podman's default container_t for the
##   qdistro Tier-2 workload (rootless podman host for a nested weston +
##   qdwin-shell.so publisher; see tier2/spawn-tier2.sh).
##
##   Tier-2 already gets strong isolation from the user namespace +
##   container_t + the launcher's runtime hardening (--cap-drop=ALL,
##   no-new-privileges, --read-only rootfs, custom seccomp, private
##   ipc/pid, per-container /run/user, --network=none). What container_t
##   does NOT give is a qdistro-specific *narrowing*: every podman
##   container on the host shares one container_t allow-set, including
##   its full unconfined-network and kernel-state-reader surface, which
##   a Tier-2 workload (default --network=none, no kernel introspection)
##   never uses. This module defines qdistro_tier2_t as a functional
##   container domain MINUS that surplus surface, capped by typebounds so
##   it can provably never exceed container_t.
##
##   Domain-construction model (IMPORTANT — read before editing):
##     qdistro_tier2_t is a member of the container_domain and
##     svirt_sandbox_domain attributes. That is what makes it a WORKING
##     container domain: those attributes carry the entrypoint / exec /
##     library-read / container_file_t / tmpfs baseline a container
##     process needs to even start (typebounds does NOT inherit
##     container_t's allows — a bounded type gets only its own rules,
##     capped at the parent). qdistro_tier2_t deliberately does NOT join
##     the network/kernel attributes container_t also carries
##     (corenet_unconfined_type, corenet_unlabeled_type,
##     container_net_domain, sandbox_net_domain, can_dump_kernel,
##     can_receive_kernel_messages, kernel_system_state_reader). Omitting
##     those is the concrete narrowing; the neverallow block at the
##     bottom pins it so a future allow can't quietly add the surface
##     back.
##
##   The launcher still uses stock container_t. This module also grants
##   that domain the narrow host socket connection edges below; loading it
##   is therefore required even while qdistro_tier2_t stays disengaged.
##
##   Build note: unlike the Tier-1 module (refpolicy m4, built through
##   /usr/share/selinux/devel/Makefile), this module is kernel policy
##   language so it compiles with the base checkmodule/semodule_package
##   toolchain the qdistro host ships WITHOUT selinux-policy-devel. The
##   host carries container-selinux (container_t, container_domain,
##   svirt_sandbox_domain, container_file_t, the container_* booleans)
##   but not the full refpolicy m4 header set, so tier1's idioms
##   (policy_module(), gen_require(), container_domain_template()) can't
##   expand here. The kernel-language `require {}` block is the direct
##   equivalent of tier1's gen_require().
##
##   See doc/selinux.md for the full architectural rationale and
##   selinux/tier2/README.md for the validated-vs-deferred split.
## </desc>
module qdistro_tier2 0.2.0;

########################################
#
# Declarations
#
# Kernel-language equivalent of tier1's gen_require(): pull in the host
# (container-selinux) types/attributes and the object classes this
# module references. Every name here was confirmed present in the live
# policy on the qdistro host (`seinfo -t`, `seinfo -a`, `seinfo -b`).

require {
	# The parent container domain. qdistro_tier2_t is bounded by it.
	type container_t;

	# The two attributes that carry the functional container baseline
	# (entrypoint, exec image rootfs, read libs, container_file_t,
	# tmpfs, etc.). Joining these is what makes the domain usable.
	attribute container_domain;
	attribute svirt_sandbox_domain;

	# MCS constraint attribute. container_t carries this; it is what
	# enforces per-container MCS-category isolation (a container can't
	# touch another's container_file_t / signal another's process
	# across categories). We MUST join it too — omitting it would
	# *broaden* cross-category access, the opposite of narrowing.
	attribute mcs_constrained_type;

	# Socket classes named only by the neverallow assertions below —
	# the surface we are pinning OFF. (These come from the network
	# attributes we deliberately do NOT join; verified that neither
	# container_domain nor svirt_sandbox_domain grants create on any of
	# them, so the neverallows can't collide with the joined baseline.)
	class tcp_socket { create listen };
	class udp_socket { create };
	class rawip_socket { create };
	class sctp_socket { create };
	class dccp_socket { create };
	class icmp_socket { create };
	class netlink_route_socket { create };
	class netlink_tcpdiag_socket { create };
	class packet_socket { create };
}

# The Tier-2 confinement domain. The nested-weston podman workload runs
# here once spawn-tier2.sh labels it (see README "Engaging the domain").
type qdistro_tier2_t;

# Functional baseline: join the container file/exec attributes so the
# domain can actually run a container. This is the deliberate,
# load-bearing difference from a from-scratch bounded type (which could
# not even exec the entrypoint). It does NOT join the network/kernel
# attributes container_t also carries — that omission is the narrowing.
typeattribute qdistro_tier2_t container_domain;
typeattribute qdistro_tier2_t svirt_sandbox_domain;
# MCS isolation must be preserved — see require{} note. Without this the
# domain would escape per-container category separation.
typeattribute qdistro_tier2_t mcs_constrained_type;

# typebounds caps qdistro_tier2_t at container_t: the kernel refuses any
# permission this domain would hold that container_t lacks (logged as
# `op=security_bounded_transition result=denied` for the
# transition/relabel edge; ordinary excess access is a plain AVC). Since
# we only join attributes that are SUBSETS of container_t's attribute
# set, the bound holds by construction. The bound is the guardrail
# against a future edit accidentally widening the domain past the
# default container surface.
typebounds container_t qdistro_tier2_t;

########################################
#
# Narrowing — what qdistro_tier2_t must NEVER hold
#
# These are the qdistro-specific constraints beyond default container_t.
# Each is a permission container_t HAS (via the network attributes) that
# the Tier-2 workload does not need under the launcher's defaults, and
# that we want pinned OFF so no later allow (here or in a child module)
# can re-grant it. neverallow is enforced at policy build/expand time,
# so a violating allow fails the load loudly rather than silently
# widening the domain.
#
# Network: Tier-2's launcher default is --network=none. container_t
# carries full unconfined networking via corenet_unconfined_type /
# sandbox_net_domain; by not joining those attributes the domain has no
# net sockets, and these neverallows guarantee it stays that way. A
# workload that genuinely needs outbound (TIER2_NETWORK=pasta)
# must run as stock container_t, not qdistro_tier2_t — an explicit,
# auditable downgrade rather than a silent allowance.
# Covers the transport + raw + diag/route socket classes that
# container_t can create via its network attributes. This is the
# practical network surface, not a claim of total coverage of every
# netlink_* subclass — a workload that needs networking should run as
# stock container_t, not qdistro_tier2_t.
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:tcp_socket { create listen };
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:udp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:rawip_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:sctp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:dccp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:icmp_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:netlink_route_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:netlink_tcpdiag_socket create;
neverallow qdistro_tier2_t self:packet_socket create;

########################################
# Desktop transport for the shipped stock-container_t launcher.
#
# The runtime tree and silo home use :Z, but the outer Wayland and
# PipeWire socket inodes are shared with host clients (including tier1).
# Never relabel them. Only the explicitly bound sockets are reachable in
# the container mount namespace: no host runtime directory is exposed.
# sock_file write authorizes connect(2), NOT regular-file writes. Do not
# add user_tmp_t file/dir management or a broad userdomain connect rule.
# Tumbleweed runs the admin compositor/secctx listener and user PipeWire
# in unconfined_t. Other server domains require a separate reviewed rule.
require {
    type user_tmp_t;
    type unconfined_t;
    class sock_file { getattr write };
    class unix_stream_socket connectto;
}
allow container_t user_tmp_t:sock_file { getattr write };
allow container_t unconfined_t:unix_stream_socket connectto;

# qdistro_tier2_t remains deferred: it needs explicit process-label wiring,
# these transport permissions bounded by container_t, and an enforcing
# workload/AVC pass. Do not engage it merely because this module loads.
# container_runtime_t (podman/conmon) remains outside this module's scope.
